CHMOD(1)                    User Commands

NAME
    chmod - change permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod [-R] mode file...

DESCRIPTION
    chmod sets the permission bits of each file. mode is an octal number
    such as 0755, or a comma separated list of clauses of the form
    [ugoa][+-=][rwxXst], which add, remove or set the read, write and
    execute bits of the owner (u), the group (g), others (o) or all (a).
    X grants execute permission only to directories and to files that are
    executable for someone already, s stands for the set user id and set
    group id bits and t for the sticky bit. A clause without u, g, o or a
    applies to all three, limited by the umask.

    -R changes directories and everything below them. Symbolic links met
    during the walk are not followed.

    Only the owner of a file and root may change its mode. The set group id
    bit is dropped when the caller does not belong to the file's group.

EXAMPLES
    chmod 0644 notes.txt
    chmod u+x,go-w script.sh
    chmod -R go= private

SEE ALSO
    chown(1), ls(1), stat(1), sh(1), index(7)
